\f4\fs26 Nothing scared Cracker more than being squashed or smothered and he was not about to get caught up in a crush now. He was desperately looking around trying to find an escape route, his mind spinning trying to grasp at any idea, but he had nothing. He considered the possibility of using his Vid-phone, but whom would he call and how would they get here? Sarah did not look like she was coming up with anything either. In an automatic response to terrorist events all bars, hotels, clubs and public buildings had now locked their doors and unless you were directly outside your residence, you were locked out. If you were locked inside a pub, you could do nothing but watch bedlam erupt, sipping a cold one. \
Cracker\'92s Vid-phone began vibrating in his pocket, but his arms were now wedged too tightly against his body to grab it quickly. He wriggled his pelvis and wedged his hip into Sarah\'92s side. \'93Grab my phone, will you?\'94\
\'93A girl would be pretty stupid falling for that one.\'94 She said, sliding her hand in his pocket nevertheless. \'93Why don\'92t you have an implant?\'94 \
\'93Too much of a pain in\'85.I was going to say backside, but it really is a pain in the head. I just don\'92t fancy a device being implanted in my brain. Besides, I don\'92t want my head cracked open every time a new model hits the market.\'94 \
\'93That is an exceedingly unenlightened view. Okay I think I have it.\'94 She removed her hand and the vid device from his pocket and opened it, holding it in front of his face.\'94 \
\'93Shakti here. Over.\'94\
\'93You don\'92t have to say \'93over\'94. It\'92s a fucking phone.\'94 Cracker said, immediately exasperated by his dopey confederate.\
\'93Okay. I forgot. Where are you? I need you for a big clean up.\'94\
\'93I\'92m stuck in a crush on the Grand Parade, outside Hardy Tower 87. You\'92ll have to get someone else.\'94\
\'93No. I will come and get you. It\'92s on my way. Expect me in five minutes.\'94\
\'93Make sure you bring 2 sets of protective gear, I\'92ve got my assistant with me.\'94\
\'93An assistant, eh? Very fancy. Over and out.\'94\
The transmission terminated abruptly and Sarah put the phone back in his pocket, hand lingering longer than was strictly necessary, but withdrawn abruptly and well before any inference could be drawn.\
 \
\'93I don\'92t know how he is going to get through this crowd, but I don\'92t think we can get out any other way.\'94\
\'93An assistant cleaner? My mother would be proud.\'94 \
\'93That\'92s all I could think of. At least I remembered the suits.\'94 \
\'93What do you want with suits?\'94\
\'93It will stop our clothes contaminating the crime scene. They\'92ve got machines that can ID gnat shit a thousand metres away and we would leave residue all over the place. It wouldn\'92t take long to track its source\'94\
\'93Yes, I should have known that.\'94 She said, acknowledging his keen judgment and scolding herself for this intellectual slip. She did not entertain the idea of any man being her intellectual equal and while he had not issued her a serious challenge, she had to admit he did have moments of clarity. It was surprising to meet a low that was bright and even more surprising to find one she liked. From her tower above, they had all seemed dirty uneducated brutes. Nothing to concern herself with and certainly not mingle with, but here she was sidling with thousands of prime examples of reeking lows. Her initial disgust was replaced by a deep sense of sympathy and guilt. 
\f5\i How many of these people have to live in squalor in order for me to live in luxury.
\f4\i0  
\f5\i I stand on many shoulders.
\f4\i0  Just as these thoughts began to settle in there was a loud commotion above. Shakti had commandeered a Hover-Copter and was descending fast. Incredibly, the crowd managed to part sufficiently for the small craft to hover a metre or so above the ground and allow a storm trooper to jump out, particle rifle at the ready. All but two of the crowd reeled and cowered in fear as the policeman approached, \'93Cleaner and assistant?\'94 They nodded. \'93Put these on and get in.\'94\
His rifle remained horizontal, pointing and shooting haphazardly at the crowd, only killing two or three to keep the rest of the horde in check.\
The protective suits were no more than white poly-paper overalls with silly hoods and booties but would conceal signs of the explosion. Face masks covered any facial injuries, of which Sarah now had none and provided welcome relief from the acrid stench. \
\'93Did you see me pop those citizens, sir? Smote them good.\'94 A grinning young constable said as he high-fived the pilot. \'93Woo, hell yeah!\'94\
\'93Yes, nice shooting constable, but did you have to kill them?\'94 asked a rather perplexed Shakti.\
\'93No, but there is no point just wounding them, it wrecks your shot to kill ratio. My stats are down by 5 per cent this month.\'94 Shakti remained a little grave and was not overly convinced of the need to summarily execute random citizens. \'93Don\'92t worry sir. I shot white people, so it wasn\'92t racist or anything.\'94 \
\'93In that case, well done and keep up the good work.\'94 Said an unconvinced Shakti.\
\'93Thank you sir. Don\'92t forget to buckle up \'96 safety first, remember?\'94 Smiled the young constable as he high-fived with pilot one more time. \'93I love this shit!\'94\
As the small craft ascended, Shakti quietly filled Cracker and Sarah in, \'93There\'92s been a coordinated terrorist attack. They let off an explosive device in a crowded club and managed to divert the water away from the fire. The city engineers are stumped and I\'92ve got to sort out the mess. We\'92re on our way to a public hygiene facility, it\'92s just up ahead.\'94 \
Cracker and Sarah glared at each other with alarm. \'93What do you want us for?\'94 stammered Cracker. Returning to the scene of the crime was not what Cracker had in mind. Sarah suddenly was not terribly concerned for the fate of the crowd below, but dreadfully afraid for her own. Adrenaline flooded their systems, hearts bouncing about madly. \
\'93C\'92mon, Cracker, you know me.\'94 Shakti said leaning in to whisper. \'93I don\'92t have a clue how to clear this up. You\'92re a cleaner. Show me how to clean.\'94 \
\'93Wait a minute.\'94 Cracker said then turned to Sarah and she nodded in silent agreement. \'93I think we might be able to help, but it will cost you. You will owe me big for this one my friend.\'94\
\'93Oh, anything. Just get met through this.\'94 He truly didn\'92t have a clue.\
\'93I am fucking serious. I will collect on this debt. In the future I will ask for something and you will deliver, no questions asked.\'94 Cracker sounded like he knew what he was saying, but in reality he was shitting himself and blurting out anything that came to mind. \
\'93Okay. I agree. Anything to get me through this. I am already in enough trouble with the Commissioner about the Lee death. He seemed to think it was murder, but it was obviously suicide, wasn\'92t it?\'94\
\'93Shit Alex, I got there after the body-snatchers. Let\'92s concentrate on the task at hand, huh?\'94 
\f5\i Lee was murdered?
\f4\i0 \
\'93Alright. Just fix this for me.\'94 Shakti was now pleading, tears welling in his dull, unremarkable eyes.\
\'93I\'92ll try. Just keep out our way and keep our names out of this.\'94 He felt odd ordering the senior cop around, but he knew him too well. If you put him in the corner and asked him not to move, things went a hell of a lot smoother. When they arrived they strode straight to the control room and were met by a swarm of engineers and computer geeks all claiming they could do nothing to stop the flow of water.\
\'93What has occurred here is impossible\'94 said the head geek. \'93Impossible.\'94\
\'93Don\'92t worry. I will have this set straight shortly. My assistants have their instructions and my full confidence. Just do what they say.\'94 Shakti assertively announced and wandered off as if to survey the scene. His career was in the hands of a cleaner and some random chick, but it was better than it being in his own. \
\'93If you gentlemen could give me a bit of room I will see what I can do.\'94 Sarah requested, but the frazzled men stood firm and mute. \'93Please leave.\'94 \
\'93It will do you no good.\'94 One said as he tramped out the door. \
\'93We\'92ll have to use explosives.\'94 Another added before disappearing from view. \
